#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189114} :activity/id #uuid "4306f994-2576-5ea8-aa86-53b75b9d993b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050682 :topic/id #uuid "5e9ef58b-352e-5161-ad27-7cfe61e49ba0" :topic/math-academy-id 3863} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188209 :question/id #uuid "e4007ddd-a5ed-5f3d-96cc-4c911d97a615" :question/math-academy-id "e-9187"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478444}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"} :step/id #uuid "57744d65-db5a-5f3d-bf13-43a012006fdf" :step/math-academy-id 34269 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188210 :question/id #uuid "1e1f0ecb-6f45-53ac-ae5d-ff953567aefa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9188"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049728}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524965}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"} :step/id #uuid "36904357-d45f-58d8-af71-db1903bb3b44" :step/math-academy-id 34272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188002 :question/id #uuid "175f3917-8801-515d-a493-5f36a52aa01b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10097"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524962}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"} :step/id #uuid "9b164372-2e9b-5b11-9a7e-f7aa6897c577" :step/math-academy-id 38081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189379 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\in A, Y\\in B)=P(X\\in A)\\cdot P(Y\\in B).\n$$\n\nIn practice, this is not a very helpful definition when checking for independence since it involves verifying that it's true for all possible subsets $A$ and $B!$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal density functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Then, it can be shown that $X$ and $Y$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all values of $x$ and $y.$ Notice that this has a direct analogy with the case of independence for discrete random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "c151ba98-beac-55c8-9a33-b3f619f0a290" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "074fbae0-d9f5-5e1f-8944-2352a5ff16a7" :step/math-academy-id 38083 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189419 :tutorial/content "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ whose joint support $S$ is rectangular, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose that the joint support of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is rectangular. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nfor some two functions $g$ and $h$ (not necessarily probability density functions), then $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Note that the rectangular joint support $S$ does not need to be finite.* \n\nFor example, let's consider the random variables $X$ and $Y$ with the following joint PDF:\n\n![](images/01/01a68d0e42bc71729543e74722390291787ce60530c2432ffa0cdf6c0bb5f093.svg)\n\nThe joint support $[0,2]\\times [0, \\infty)$ is rectangular. Also, we can write $f(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y),$ where\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac14 x, \\qquad h(y) = e^{-y/2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent." :tutorial/id #uuid "9e6987fa-0133-5b85-b6a5-e58a401020b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6534 :tutorial/title "Independence When the Joint Support Is Rectangular"} :step/id #uuid "260c1a0b-a4ec-5618-9cb3-1bfc17d53a21" :step/math-academy-id 39479}] :activity/title "Independence of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188458}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525144} :activity/id #uuid "0a3d1120-832a-5700-ba94-05d6bb26c8cd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050682 :topic/id #uuid "5e9ef58b-352e-5161-ad27-7cfe61e49ba0" :topic/math-academy-id 3863} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a10c141-494b-5ffe-b789-4d235572d460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524600} {:answer/id #uuid "94c5f700-7024-5ad9-9fb8-06de616c6958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524619} {:answer/id #uuid "a350e58a-9fe9-5a48-a78c-f6ab313c004a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524643} {:answer/id #uuid "f085a1c6-cfcd-5341-bcfa-3848ba092d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524805} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb5fdb8-bd3a-507b-bfd0-0f58c8d15589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524619} :answer-field/id #uuid "54963578-cc23-5bef-b243-20d224d918a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "02ed11c8-435c-5d94-883f-47ba8fa464eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143377" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{2}{3}y$ for $y\\in [1,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [1,2].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [0,1].$ So, when $x\\in [0,1]$ and $y\\in [1,2],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{8}{3}{x}^{3}y}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}={{(4{x}^{3})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{(\\frac{2}{3}y)}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\""} :step/id #uuid "3d7ed4a8-d1ca-5534-8d7d-2245f866ff37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525101}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22b3a3fc-340b-5ed5-8d5e-990986db2466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524444} {:answer/id #uuid "411a9cec-3a8e-59f5-9ad3-e086842f889a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524502} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca1210b-5ee2-5865-924d-eb975f41a198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524559} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6c7f06-285b-5716-8654-ada585b5cad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524608} {:answer/id #uuid "e274eb34-e730-5088-9d44-3c0f08b82dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524608} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b00f6e3-8cc3-5080-b638-0c0a437c40d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1706fef5-2c32-54fe-bdcf-eb9228266f86" :question/math-academy-id "q-143364" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y, & \\,3\\le y\\le 5, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,\\infty )\\times [3,5].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}, & \\,0\\le x,\\,3\\le y\\le 5 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2,3\\le Y\\le 4) & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{8}y{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{-x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{3}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{b}\\frac{7}{16}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{7{e}^{-x}}{16}]}_{2}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{7{e}^{-b}}{16}+\\frac{7{e}^{-2}}{16}] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4cb124b6-2f91-5b80-98ad-73768b8d6164" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525101 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310a997c-e1ee-509f-b5a5-c83f303016e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524471} {:answer/id #uuid "96855423-2c14-53c8-9841-2610cea6223a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524621} {:answer/id #uuid "afa9fea7-5a60-5e00-be15-b60751c4ec57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524665} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d3546d-16f4-553d-bc58-12b528100c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of these listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524779} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b9033f-aefa-5a72-bc8f-61f04664c8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524806}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524665} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e41b8cd-f547-56b5-b624-9d4998a39501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "09b415e6-d89a-5c4c-91d7-faeef6a95b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-143387" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y),\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})$ for $y\\in [2,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+yx]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2+2y-\\frac{1}{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $y\\in [2,3].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the marginal density function for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=2}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+\\frac{9}{2}-2x-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x\\in [1,2].$ So, when $x\\in [1,2]$ and $y\\in [2,3],$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n{{\\frac{1}{4}(x+y)}_{⏟}}_{f(x,y)}\\ne {{\\frac{1}{4}(x+\\frac{5}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{X}(x)}\\cdot {{\\frac{1}{4}(y+\\frac{3}{2})}_{⏟}}_{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is *not* true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "6e0cb841-b7e4-52d1-9070-be7f75eefde3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9efbc4f-37b6-5dfc-a3af-79eea3983163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.238" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185647} {:answer/id #uuid "041e0497-134b-5de8-bc9b-1ea1cc88a289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.345" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185648} {:answer/id #uuid "16bccea1-d36b-5c0f-b068-3b702c40c2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.412" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185649} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9f9576-00aa-5ad8-b83c-03c6b02f7706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.253" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185650} {:answer/id #uuid "79aa0e52-c2f0-5438-9e88-2676450da90c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.423" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185650} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf35cc72-3db3-5a32-afbf-f173e83fb7fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "01f303ca-e9ff-5d45-9ae0-7430f5f2b5fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-143352" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2).$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}y \\\\  & =y\\sqrt{x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,2].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y\\sqrt{x}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 0.4,Y\\le 2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}[{\\int }_{1}^{2}y\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\sqrt{x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =(0.4)^{3/2}-0 \\\\  & ≈0.253.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "829bd28e-43ba-5401-9217-1577c3b42966" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f79756-9008-5db9-a58e-f4dda6d20cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185491} {:answer/id #uuid "87633653-db83-5aff-b7bd-240bf6394e52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185492} {:answer/id #uuid "d81e8b14-3433-54d9-8503-3e8daadbce31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185493} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0eea0e-e9ed-598c-a0ce-d2a51d1ad5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185494} {:answer/id #uuid "6f95668f-f5da-5049-abb0-85b1ac006401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185493} :answer-field/id #uuid "186c6e8a-c524-5e91-8fb3-a11cea8703df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0111779d-e047-5622-b502-1438439ba7f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-110390" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =6xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}6xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}6x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f08653cb-bc58-5a7a-a441-4c86bbb7eea7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525107}}] :activity/title "Independence of Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524362}]}
